WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
Starlink secured an internet provider license in Bosnia and Herzegovina, opening service rollout in the coming weeks. This is SpaceX Daily, episode 115. The communications regulatory agency issued the license to Starlink director Rebecca Slick Hunter, after months of negotiations. Starlink BH was incorporated locally as part of the process. Service is scheduled to begin in the coming weeks. The approval marks the latest European expansion for the constellation.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.440
The regulatory step follows the official issuance of an Internet provider license by the

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.080
communications regulatory agency to the Starlink Director.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:45.040
Negotiations involved the local incorporation of the subsidiary before the license was granted.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:50.300
According to telecom, PAPRA, the license allows Starlink to operate as an Internet provider

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.080
in the country.

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:57.040
NASA is investing in Boeing's Starliner to support astronaut flights into the 2030s.

00:00:57.040 --> 00:01:03.580
The agency is also courting additional commercial vendors to develop successors to the SpaceX Dragon capsule.

00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:09.400
SpaceX plans to phase out Dragon and Falcon 9 as it shifts resources to Starship.

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:16.640
NASA Administrator Jared Isaacman outlined the approach during a Monday press conference on Starliner's return to flight.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:21.620
The agency states it does not yet know the exact timeline for Sunsetting Dragon,

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.980
but is already working to maintain an American human spaceflight capability.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:30.460
Additionally, additional vendors would need to demonstrate reliable crew transport before

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:31.380
any transition.

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:37.620
The approach reflects both execution risk on existing vehicles and a desire for redundancy.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:43.420
Space Axe has picked up the load for NASA while Boeing struggled to address Starliner's thruster

00:01:43.420 --> 00:01:45.060
malfunctions and helium leaks.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:51.920
Starship Flight 14 reached orbit for the first time and deployed 26 Starlink V3 satellites.

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:56.640
lights. One engine shut down during ascent, leading controllers to shorten the flight

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:02.100
and forego booster recovery. The demonstrated launch capacity supports planned large satellite

00:02:02.100 --> 00:02:07.980
fleets, including star shield and missile defense architectures. The uncrewed rocket overcame

00:02:07.980 --> 00:02:13.220
the early shutdown of one engine to complete its first orbital burn. Starship released the

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:19.640
26 satellites one at a time at roughly 170 miles altitude. The super heavy booster made

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:24.720
an engine-controlled splashdown in the Gulf of Mexico and was not recovered while starship

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:30.740
splashdown in the northern Pacific. Each starlink V3 satellite is designed for one terabit

00:02:30.740 --> 00:02:38.980
per second downlink and 160 gigabits per second uplink capacity. 6400 gigabit space lasers per satellite

00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:46.200
enable high bandwidth links across a petabit mesh network. 4 quadband radio frequency backhaul antennas

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:54.460
support KA, EV, and W frequencies. Next-generation beamformer chips increase throughput per modem chip

00:02:54.460 --> 00:03:00.560
by a factor of 64. The greater data density and number of beams from the new phased arrays

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:06.680
are enabled by the next generation of SpaceX developed beamformer chips. After deploying from

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:12.480
Starship, the satellites unfold their antennas and deploy solar arrays before raising orbits with

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:13.460
on-board thrusters.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:17.720
Observers noted two static features on the Flight 14 stream.

00:03:18.360 --> 00:03:23.680
One elongated feature and one possibly lunar in scale remained visible for an extended

00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:25.180
portion of the Ascent coverage.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:28.180
Community discussion continues on identification.

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:33.800
The one in the red circle is long and thin, while the one in the yellow circle could be the

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.460
moon but looks a little small.

00:03:36.100 --> 00:03:40.740
The features are both clearly some distance off from the ship and seem to be fairly static.

00:03:40.740 --> 00:03:44.640
The satellites measure 7 meters by 3.5 meters.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:50.040
Deploying long-side first would require a 7 meters payload bay door plus margins.

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:56.700
Short-side first would allow a narrower 3.5 meters door, potentially improving structural margins.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:00.960
The satellite has dimensions of 7 meters by 3.5 meters.

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.680
To deploy it, long-side first requires a payload bay door of width 7 meters plus margins.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:12.100
The rear docking port showed significant thermal effects on the leeward side, while the

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:13.540
opposite port remained cool.

00:04:14.500 --> 00:04:18.840
Observers suggested the banking maneuver may have produced asymmetric plasma flow.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:24.780
The rear starship docking port got toasty during reentry, with intense localized heating

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.840
on the leeward side from plasma flow.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:31.000
The opposite docking port showed virtually zero thermal effects.

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.780
Starbucks. Starships demonstrated orbital performance is cited as the inflection point

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:41.280
for the $200 per kilogram threshold. Lower launch costs would enable orbital compute

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:47.520
architectures previously considered an economic. The break even point at $200 per kilogram

00:04:47.880 --> 00:04:53.620
marks the point where space data centers become viable. Starship success marks the inflection

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:59.840
point for these economics. Observers in the SpaceX lounge subreddit discussed possible identification

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:04.000
of static objects visible on the starship launch stream near the horizon.

00:05:04.780 --> 00:05:09.200
One elongated feature and one smaller circular object prompted speculation,

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.840
ranging from distant hardware to the moon.

00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:15.240
The thread remains active with additional frame analysis.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:20.720
Users examined frame grabs from the YouTube stream at the 506 second mark.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.000
The discussion includes multiple theories on whether the objects are the moon

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:26.500
or something else entirely.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:33.500
users examined why Starlink V3 satellites are deployed long-sided first despite the narrower door

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:39.840
that would be possible with short-side orientation. The seven meters by 3.5 meters dimensions and

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:45.240
resulting payload bay requirements formed the core of the discussion. Contributors noted that a

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:50.180
smaller door could be better structurally, yet the design still uses the long-side approach.

00:05:50.740 --> 00:05:55.860
The thread explores what the team might be missing in the structural trade-offs. A separate post

00:05:55.860 --> 00:06:01.180
highlighted asymmetric heating on the rear docking port during reentry, with one side showing

00:06:01.180 --> 00:06:06.680
intense localized effects, while the opposite port stayed cool. Contributors link the

00:06:06.680 --> 00:06:11.860
pattern to the vehicle's banking maneuver and plasma flow. The post includes a frame showing

00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:17.460
the rear port getting significantly hotter on one side. Discussion centers on whether the maneuver

00:06:17.460 --> 00:06:23.480
caused the severe asymmetric heating. One thing worth watching is NASA's decision to fund Boeing

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:29.240
Starliner and solicit new commercial crew vehicles while SpaceX prepares to retire Dragon.

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.980
On the AI front, Space Data Center break-even economics are now cited at $200 per kilogram

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:42.080
delivered to orbit. Starships' first orbital flight is presented as the threshold that makes such

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:48.360
architectures viable. Lower per kilogram costs would shift the trade space for orbital compute nodes

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:50.920
that rely on Starlink class backhaul.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:53.960
The analysis ties directly to ongoing work

00:06:53.960 --> 00:06:56.000
on space-based AI infrastructure.

00:06:56.720 --> 00:06:59.260
The greater data density from V3 satellites

00:06:59.260 --> 00:07:02.160
supports the backhaul needed for orbital compute nodes.

00:07:02.780 --> 00:07:04.140
From an engineering standpoint,

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:06.020
the decision to continue to orbit

00:07:06.020 --> 00:07:08.720
after a single raptor vacuum engine shut down

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:11.300
during a send reveals how the vehicle's

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:13.900
propulsion architecture absorbs partial failures.

00:07:14.600 --> 00:07:18.080
The remaining five RVAC engines extended their burn time

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:23.040
to compensate for the lost thrust, completing the orbital insertion burn on schedule.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.280
This approach trades a shortened mission duration for the ability to reach the target velocity

00:07:28.280 --> 00:07:34.780
with one engine out. The mass of the 26 starlink V3 satellites plus the upper stage itself

00:07:34.780 --> 00:07:40.640
sets a clear propellant budget. Extending burn time on the surviving engines consumes additional

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:46.500
propellant but avoids an immediate abort. The same margin that allowed the shortened three-hour flight

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:52.500
instead of the planned 10-hours, also preserve the option to deorbit safely into the pre-cleared Pacific

00:07:52.500 --> 00:08:00.820
zone. On the payload side, each V3 satellites six 400 gigabit lasers and four quad-band RF antennas

00:08:00.820 --> 00:08:06.920
represent a step change in on-orbit data routing density. The 64-fold increase in throughput

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:12.340
per modem chip directly reduces the number of satellites needed to serve a given user density,

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:15.900
lowering the mass that must be lifted per bit of capacity.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:21.480
When the raw material cost of silicon, propellant, and structure is compared with the delivered

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:26.700
service, the idiot index for these satellites is already falling because the same launch

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:32.240
vehicle can now place ten times the downlink capacity that a Falcon 9 previously delivered.

00:08:32.820 --> 00:08:37.640
The next design iteration will likely focus on further reducing that index by increasing

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:42.760
laser power, or modem integration density, rather than simply adding more satellites.

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:47.780
A quick look at the markets shows the stock moved lower by a fraction of a percent.

00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.280
Before we go, the next regulatory step for Starlink in additional European markets could

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:53.940
come soon.

